            ("Right Target — Causal Disease Biology & Genetic Anchor", "Wallerian motor axonolysis is triggered post-translationally when distal axonal NMNAT2 depletion elevates the NMN/NAD+ ratio and unlocks SARM1's autoinhibitory ARM ring — a distal execution step invisible to cortical soma bulk RNA-seq. Human ALS rare-variant cohorts enrich for constitutively active SARM1 ARM gain-of-function alleles, while gnomAD v4.1 MANE shows no strong population depletion of heterozygous predicted loss-of-function variation (ENSG00000004139, MANE ENST00000585482.6, LOEUF = 1.413 [60/52.6]; Open Targets / legacy gene constraint LOEUF = 1.143; distinct from clinical biallelic knockout phenotyping)."),
            ("Right Tissue — Multi-Omic Human Pathology & Assay Nuance", "Small-sample C9orf72-FTD (1/10 FTD-MND) iPSC neurons show elevated SARM1 protein (+0.675 log2FC, p=0.0268, n=3 vs 3; Spillman et al. 2026, PMID:42789089, PXD083082), whereas end-stage NYGC frontal bulk RNA (-0.307 log2FC, p=1.24e-6; GSE153960) reflects terminal projection-neuron dropout. Although STMN2 and NMNAT2 mRNA co-vary across BA4 excitatory neurons (r=+0.9284, p=1.95e-14; Pineda et al. 2024, PMID:38521060), Stmn2 depletion did not lower axonal NMNAT2 or activate SARM1 in mouse motor axons (Krus et al. 2022, PMID:35767949), positioning SARM1 and STMN2 within a two-branch competing-hazard axonal architecture."),
            ("Right Modality & Exposure — 3-Pillar PK/PD, Selectivity & Trial Landscape", "Cryo-EM structure PDB 9TZY (Lundback et al. 2026, PMID:42156967: BEXi adduct 17 / A1JZ0) and 122-scaffold profiling (Leahey et al. 2025, PMID:42380237) prove orthosteric base-exchange (BEXi) prodrugs bridge three TIR chains and paradoxically activate SARM1 below IC50 across all 122/122 tested compounds (7 chemical series; median 8.6-fold [IQR 5.1–9.7] IC50/C_act concentration separation in Fig. 2g; modeled amplitude spans ~2.8x low-reserve L0=2.5 to ~5x ALS / ~45x WT theoretical MWC ceilings [MODEL_PRIOR]). Clinical SARM1 testing spans four clinical-stage small-molecule candidates across three sponsors — Disarm-lineage BEXi prodrug Lilly LY3873862 (completed HV SAD/MAD NCT05492201, N=84; Phase 1 ALS NCT07883590 Not Yet Recruiting; PMID:33406435), Nura Bio's reversible orthosteric NB-4746 (ALS Phase 1b/2a NCT07369076) plus Phase 1 healthy-volunteer covalent allosteric NB-9402 (ACTRN12626000650392, WO2025090514A1), and Sironax's undisclosed oral allosteric inhibitor SIR2501 (CTR20253552 China ALS Ph1b/2a, NCT07765446 US paclitaxel CIPN Ph2 n=18, ANZCTR 387449 HV SAD/MAD N=82 up to 600 mg/150 mg QD, ASCO 2026 10.1200/JCO.2026.44.16_suppl.12148, SfN 2026 PSTR091; WO2024125624A1/WO2024222835A1/WO2026086928A1) — alongside Asha Therapeutics' preclinical 'intramolecular glue' ASHA-624 (0 clinical trials, undisclosed pocket/structure) and Scripps covalent Cys311 tryptoline acrylamides (EV-99, Feldman et al. 2022 PMID:35994671, WO2023235719A1). Structurally anchored to PDB 7ANW/7DJT, this analysis prioritizes reversible non-covalent inter-protomer ARM(A)–TIR(H) T-locks (verifying Cys311-independent binding via Cys311Ala SARM1 vs covalent Cys311 electrophiles in PDB 7DJT PMID:33944777 & EV-99 PMID:35994671) across 48–72 h simulated oral washout curves (5x -> 0.03x IC50).\n\nCrucially, whereas Sarm1 deletion rescues motor axons in TDP-43Q331K mice (White et al. 2019 PMID:31661035) and TBI-induced pTDP-43 / TBI-accelerated SOD1G93A models (Dogan et al. 2023/2025 PMID:38124145, PMID:39791335), Collins et al. 2022 (PMID:35863521) demonstrated that Sarm1 knockout in uninjured mSOD1G93A ALS mice significantly lowered Simoa serum NfL and GFAP (16–20 wks) and protected sciatic axons and spinal VGluT2+ excitatory puncta WITHOUT rescuing lumbar motor neuron somata, symptom onset, or 20-week survival — establishing both a non-SOD1 TDP-43-spectrum enrollment gate and a soma-synapse decoupling warning that blood NfL reduction reflects anti-axonolytic target engagement rather than standalone clinical surrogacy (requiring MScanFit MUNE / distal CMAP + bridged CSF NPTX2 ahead of Stage 2 splice combination). Furthermore, because HV circulating plasma cADPR (ASCO #12148) is estimated at >90–95% ecto-CD38/BST1-generated [MODEL_PRIOR, extrapolated from murine Cd38/Sarm1 tissue/nerve knockouts in PMID:32087251 & PMID:16750163 — capping 90% selective SARM1 blockade at ~5–10% cell-free fluid drop, so a -50% plasma drop implies ~45–50% off-target CD38 blockade] and CIPN DRGs lie outside the BBB, Phase 1b PD mandates enzymatic CD38 counter-screening, unbound human ALS CSF Ctrough,u >= IC80 (Kpuu >= 0.30), zero washout CSF pNfH/plasma NfL spike, and exploratory L1CAM/ATP1A3+ neuron-derived EV cADPR:NAD+."),
            ("Right Patient — Biomarker Stratification & Combinatorial Positioning", "Combinatorial positioning with intrathecal splice rescue (#4 C006 + #5 C002 + genotype-stratified #3 C001) rests on a non-redundant two-branch competing-hazard architecture: in Branch A (alpha=0), Sarm1 deletion does not rescue Stmn2-null motor neuropathy in double-mutant mice (Krus et al. 2022, PMID:35767949; Lopez-Erauskin et al. 2024, PMID:37996528) and STMN2 normally promotes plus-end microtubule dynamicity/catastrophe while suppressing runaway tubulin polymerization — so STMN2 loss reduces catastrophe and impairs neurite outgrowth/regeneration via a SARM1-independent mechanism (Matson & Holzbaur 2026 [PREPRINT]; 1.0x SARM1 potency shift, proving SARM1 blockade cannot substitute for STMN2 rescue), whereas Branch B (alpha in (0, 1]) captures conditional vesicular NMNAT2 co-transport stress in human iPSC-MNs (Beccari et al. 2025, PMID:40392845; 1.73–7.22x [med 2.98x] MWC floor-dose sparing [MODEL_PRIOR]).\n\nAcross full-length cryo-EM octamers (7ANW/7DJT, alongside activation comparators 7NAL/9TZY) and gnomAD v4.1, functionally validated ALS GoF alleles total ~0.076% carriers (Gilley 2021 PMID:34796871; Bloom 2022 PMID:34991663; vs benign P332Q AF=0.986%, 97 homozygotes) and split into Class I inter-protomer ARM–TIR latch breakers (E252K 3.07A, R249C 3.09A; yielding a [MODEL_PRIOR] ~36% Binomial hetero-octamer residual NADase floor at 100x Kd) and Class II Cys311-wall deformers (L223P, del226-232 AC=52, R267W AC=137 near Arg224/Glu264 — risking direct Kd shift on Cys311-site binders vs TIR orthosteric NB-4746), with SARM1 knockdown ASO backup (Loreto et al. 2025, PMID:40473123) designed outside the GENCODE v46 3' opposite-strand SLC46A1 folate-transporter overlap."),
